      "context": "I thought I was losing my sanity because, far too often, when I arrived\nat the preschool I ran, I would find the toy cabinet wide open and toys strewn\neverywhere. The rule was that each child could take out one toy at a time and\nhad to return it before getting another. High up and latched securely, the\ncabinet was not easily accessible, so I assumed a teacher with access to the     5\nkey was opening it for them. I raised the issue with the staff, but they denied\nopening it. Frustrated, I bought a combination lock and shared the code with\nthe teachers who needed access. The first week after installing it, we were\nrelieved to find the cabinet still securely locked.\n    But first thing on Monday of the following week, the cabinet had been     10\nopened again, with toys scattered everywhere. I questioned everyone who\nwas given the lock combination but each insisted that they had not opened it\nor shared the code. Assuming I had failed to lock it properly, I made sure it\nwas securely locked that day. However, the very next day, the cabinet was\nwide open again, and toys filled the room once more. It was infuriating and I     15\nconsidered nailing it shut and banning toys altogether. That day, I finally\nrealised the cause of the problem when we gathered the toys. Except those\nthe children were playing with, I put the rest away into the cabinet.\n    In my flustered state, I locked the cabinet again, forgetting that some\ntoys were still outside. Realising my mistake, I decided to leave it locked until     20\nthe children were ready to switch toys. When a little girl asked to exchange\nher toy, a three-year-old boy named Scotty, with the vocabulary of an English\nprofessor, offered to help her. I just smiled, thinking he could not open the\ncabinet. After letting Scotty and the little girl each choose a toy, I put the rest\naway into the cabinet and locked it again. When Scotty offered to help another     25\nchild exchange toys, I decided to observe. To my astonishment, he stepped\non a stool to reach the lock. Next, he confidently spun the dials of the lock and\nthe cabinet swung wide open.\n    \"Scotty, how did you learn to open that?\" I gasped. \"It was quite\nelementary,\" Scotty said with a grin. \"I analysed your movements and     30\ndeduced the combination.\"\n    I could only shake my head in disbelief. When his mother arrived to\npick him up, I told her about the incident and asked if a keyed lock might be a\nbetter option. She laughed. \"We tried that, and he figured out how to pick\nevery one we've purchased.  That's why all our locks are up high, though he     35\nstill finds ways to get to them.\" I was shocked, and asked if there was anything\nelse he did that I should know about.\n    \"Plenty of things,\" she said with a laugh. \"For instance, my husband\ngave Scotty an old clock he could not fix, thinking it would keep him busy.\nScotty spent the day tinkering, with pieces scattered everywhere. By evening,     40\nmy husband was stunned to find the clock not only reassembled but working\nperfectly, though I was not surprised in the least. Scotty has always had a\nknack for figuring things out. So, good luck keeping him out of the cabinet and\nlet me know if you figure out how.\"\n    But I never did. I stood there, torn between disbelief and admiration,     45\nrealising that sometimes the most unexpected answers come from the\nsmallest hands.\n\nA Very Precocious Child by D. W. Howard",
