      "passage": "I thought I was losing my sanity because, far too often, when I arrived at the preschool I ran, I would find the toy cabinet wide open and toys strewn everywhere. The rule was that each child could take out one toy at a time and had to return it before getting another. High up and latched securely, the cabinet was not easily accessible, so I assumed a teacher with access to the key was opening it for them. I raised the issue with the staff, but they denied opening it. Frustrated, I bought a combination lock and shared the code with the teachers who needed access. The first week after installing it, we were relieved to find the cabinet still securely locked.\n\nBut first thing on Monday of the following week, the cabinet had been opened again, with toys scattered everywhere. I questioned everyone who was given the lock combination but each insisted that they had not opened it or shared the code. Assuming I had failed to lock it properly, I made sure it was securely locked that day. However, the very next day, the cabinet was wide open again, and toys filled the room once more. It was infuriating and I considered nailing it shut and banning toys altogether. That day, I finally realised the cause of the problem when we gathered the toys. Except those the children were playing with, I put the rest away into the cabinet.\n\nIn my flustered state, I locked the cabinet again, forgetting that some toys were still outside. Realising my mistake, I decided to leave it locked until the children were ready to switch toys. When a little girl asked to exchange her toy, a three-year-old boy named Scotty, with the vocabulary of an English professor, offered to help her. I just smiled, thinking he could not open the cabinet. After letting Scotty and the little girl each choose a toy, I put the rest away into the cabinet and locked it again. When Scotty offered to help another child exchange toys, I decided to observe. To my astonishment, he stepped on a stool to reach the lock. Next, he confidently spun the dials of the lock and the cabinet swung wide open.\n\n\"Scotty, how did you learn to open that?\" I gasped. \"It was quite elementary,\" Scotty said with a grin. \"I analysed your movements and deduced the combination.\"\n\nI could only shake my head in disbelief. When his mother arrived to pick him up, I told her about the incident and asked if a keyed lock might be a better option. She laughed. \"We tried that, and he figured out how to pick every one we've purchased. That's why all our locks are up high, though he still finds ways to get to them.\" I was shocked, and asked if there was anything else he did that I should know about.\n\n\"Plenty of things,\" she said with a laugh. \"For instance, my husband gave Scotty an old clock he could not fix, thinking it would keep him busy. Scotty spent the day tinkering, with pieces scattered everywhere. By evening, my husband was stunned to find the clock not only reassembled but working perfectly, though I was not surprised in the least. Scotty has always had a knack for figuring things out. So, good luck keeping him out of the cabinet and let me know if you figure out how.\"\n\nBut I never did. I stood there, torn between disbelief and admiration, realising that sometimes the most unexpected answers come from the smallest hands.\n\nA Very Precocious Child by D. W. Howard",
